spec:
  background: 7 (orange)
  separator: 6 (magenta)
  blocks:
    count: 3-4
    size: 5x5 each
    sprite: single connected component, 1 non-bg color per block, 4-10 fg pixels
    colors: each block has one color from {1-5,8,9}; blocks may share colors
  chain:
    orientation: exactly one of H or V admits a valid chain (not both)
    edge_contact: each connecting edge has exactly 1 fg pixel at some offset;
      consecutive blocks share that offset (intersection size = 1)
    endpoints: first block has 0 fg on outer leading edge; last block has 0 fg on outer trailing edge
    perpendicular_edges: all blocks have 0 fg pixels on the two edges perpendicular to the chain direction
  input_layout: one of 2x2 grid (4 blocks), V-strip, or H-strip; blocks scrambled (order != chain order)
  output_layout: 1xN strip (H or V) in chain order, separated by single sep lines

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick N=3 or 4 and chain orientation (H or V).
    2. For each consecutive pair, pick a shared edge-contact offset (0-4). Assign each block
       0, 1, or 2 connecting edges depending on position (endpoints get 1, interior get 2).
    3. Generate N 5x5 sprites on bg=7, each single-connected single-color, with exactly the
       required fg pixels touching chain-direction edges at designated offsets, and zero fg
       on perpendicular edges. Ensure no accidental perpendicular-edge fg.
    4. Verify the chain is unique (exactly one orientation works, exactly one permutation).
       Reject if ambiguous or if the non-chain orientation also admits a valid chain.
    5. Assemble output as 1xN strip with sep=6 dividers.
    6. Scramble block order and arrange into input layout: for N=4 use 2x2 grid or strip;
       for N=3 use strip. Ensure input order differs from chain order.
       Choose input orientation to differ from output when using strip layout [observed].
